Design of an Optimized Linux File System Layout
Aim:
To design an efficient Linux file system layout for a 50 GB server that manages system logs, user files, and application data while preventing disk space exhaustion
Problem Analysis:
System log files can grow rapidly.
Users need space for their files.
Applications require storage for data.
If the root partition becomes full, critical services may stop working.
Therefore, separate partitions or logical volumes should be used so that excessive log growth does not affect the entire system.
Proposed Storage Layout:
/boot` – 1 GB: Stores boot files and the Linux kernel.
`/` – 8 GB: Stores the operating system and important system files.
`/var/log` – 10 GB: Stores system and application log files. This ensures logs do not exceed 10 GB.
`/home` – 12 GB: Stores user files and personal data.
`/opt` – 14 GB: Stores application software and application data.
`swap` – 2 GB: Used as virtual memory when RAM is full.
Free Space – 3 GB: Reserved for future storage expansion.
Total Storage = 50 GB
Total = 50 GB
Partitioning Technique
Use LVM (Logical Volume Manager).
Advantages:
Partition sizes can be increased later.
Free space can be allocated when needed.
Storage management becomes flexible.
Reduces the risk of complete disk failure due to one directory growing too much.
Log Management:
The /var/log partition is limited to 10 GB.
Use logrotate to automatically manage old log files.
Example configuration:
/var/log/*.log {
    weekly
    rotate 4
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
}
This configuration:
Rotates logs every week.
Keeps only 4 old log files.
Compresses old logs.
Removes unnecessary empty logs.
Thus, log files are controlled and do not continuously consume disk space.
Disk Space Monitoring:
The following command can be used to check disk usage:
df -h
To check the size of log files:
du -sh /var/log/*
Administrators can regularly monitor disk usage and take action before partitions become full.
 Benefits of the Design:
Log files cannot consume more than the allocated 10 GB partition.
User files are separated in /home.
Application data is separated in /opt.
The operating system remains protected in the root / partition.
LVM allows future storage expansion.
Log rotation automatically removes or compresses old logs.
Critical services can continue running even if one storage area becomes full.
Result:
An optimized Linux file system layout was designed for a 50 GB server using separate partitions and LVM. The /var/log partition is restricted to 10 GB, and logrotate is used to manage log growth. This design improves disk utilization, prevents disk space exhaustion, and helps critical services continue operating without interruption.

