Process Management Strategy for High CPU Usage
Aim:
To identify and control a faulty process consuming **90% CPU** without restarting the Linux system and without affecting critical services.
1. Identify the Faulty Process
Use:
bash id="kx3lqz"
top
or:
bash id="1p5g3j"
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head
These commands show the processes using the highest CPU.
2. Monitor the Process
Suppose the faulty process ID is **1234**.
Use:
bash id="wgx10mg"
top -p 1234
This monitors only that process.
3. Reduce Process Priority
Before stopping the process, reduce its CPU priority:
bash id="e08mj8"
renice 19 -p 1234
This gives higher priority to other important processes.
4. Stop the Faulty Process Safely
First, send a normal termination signal:
bash id="vutkyo"
kill 1234
Wait and check whether the process has stopped.
If it does not stop, use:
bash id="4lq1iv"
kill -9 1234
5. Verify Critical Services
Check running services:
bash id="3d8drd"
systemctl status service_name
Check overall CPU usage again:
bash id="cj5qiu"
top
Result:
The faulty process is identified using `top` or `ps`, monitored using `top -p`, and controlled using `renice` and `kill`. This reduces CPU usage without restarting the system and helps ensure that critical services continue running.
